    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old woman presents after she had 4 falls in the past year. She has hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and takes 6 medications daily. According to the AGS/BGS Clinical Practice Guidelines, which of the following components should be included in a comprehensive multifactorial fall risk assessment?', '["Only a brain MRI to rule out a structural lesion causing the falls", "Only a medication count; falls are always caused by polypharmacy and no other evaluation is needed", "Gait and balance assessment (e.g., Timed Up and Go), orthostatic blood pressure measurement, visual acuity testing, medication review (especially CNS-active drugs, antihypertensives), lower extremity strength and sensation testing, footwear evaluation, cognitive screening, and home hazard assessment", "Only an ECG to evaluate for cardiac arrhythmias as the sole cause of falls", "No assessment is needed for community-dwelling older adults with recurrent falls, as falls are a normal part of aging"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **AGS/BGS Clinical Practice Guidelines for Fall Prevention** recommend a **multifactorial fall risk assessment** for all older adults who report **>= 2 falls in 12 months, a single fall with injury, or gait/balance difficulties**. The assessment should include: (1) **Gait, balance, and mobility** (Timed Up and Go [TUG], Berg Balance Scale), (2) **Orthostatic blood pressure** (supine to standing), (3) **Visual acuity** (cataracts, macular degeneration, glaucoma), (4) **Medication review** (especially **CNS-active drugs**: benzodiazepines, opioids, anticonvulsants, antidepressants; antihypertensives causing orthostasis), (5) **Lower extremity strength, proprioception, and sensation** (peripheral neuropathy), (6) **Footwear evaluation**, (7) **Cognitive screening** (dementia increases fall risk 2-3x), (8) **Home safety/hazard assessment** (loose rugs, poor lighting, absence of grab bars), (9) **Cardiovascular assessment** (arrhythmias, carotid sinus hypersensitivity). (Ref: AGS/BGS 2010 Clinical Practice Guideline; Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old woman presents after she fell 3 times in the past year. She has hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and takes 12 medications daily. According to the AGS/BGS Clinical Practice Guidelines, which of the following components should be included in a comprehensive multifactorial fall risk assessment?', '["Only an ECG to evaluate for cardiac arrhythmias as the sole cause of falls", "No assessment is needed for community-dwelling older adults with recurrent falls, as falls are a normal part of aging", "Only a medication count; falls are always caused by polypharmacy and no other evaluation is needed", "Gait and balance assessment (e.g., Timed Up and Go), orthostatic blood pressure measurement, visual acuity testing, medication review (especially CNS-active drugs, antihypertensives), lower extremity strength and sensation testing, footwear evaluation, cognitive screening, and home hazard assessment", "Only a brain MRI to rule out a structural lesion causing the falls"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **AGS/BGS Clinical Practice Guidelines for Fall Prevention** recommend a **multifactorial fall risk assessment** for all older adults who report **>= 2 falls in 12 months, a single fall with injury, or gait/balance difficulties**. The assessment should include: (1) **Gait, balance, and mobility** (Timed Up and Go [TUG], Berg Balance Scale), (2) **Orthostatic blood pressure** (supine to standing), (3) **Visual acuity** (cataracts, macular degeneration, glaucoma), (4) **Medication review** (especially **CNS-active drugs**: benzodiazepines, opioids, anticonvulsants, antidepressants; antihypertensives causing orthostasis), (5) **Lower extremity strength, proprioception, and sensation** (peripheral neuropathy), (6) **Footwear evaluation**, (7) **Cognitive screening** (dementia increases fall risk 2-3x), (8) **Home safety/hazard assessment** (loose rugs, poor lighting, absence of grab bars), (9) **Cardiovascular assessment** (arrhythmias, carotid sinus hypersensitivity). (Ref: AGS/BGS 2010 Clinical Practice Guideline; Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics)'),
