    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old female presents after she had 4 falls in the past year. She has hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and takes 10 medications daily. According to the AGS/BGS Clinical Practice Guidelines, which of the following components should be included in a comprehensive multifactorial fall risk assessment?', '["Only an ECG to evaluate for cardiac arrhythmias as the sole cause of falls", "Only a medication count; falls are always caused by polypharmacy and no other evaluation is needed", "Gait and balance assessment (e.g., Timed Up and Go), orthostatic blood pressure measurement, visual acuity testing, medication review (especially CNS-active drugs, antihypertensives), lower extremity strength and sensation testing, footwear evaluation, cognitive screening, and home hazard assessment", "No assessment is needed for community-dwelling older adults with recurrent falls, as falls are a normal part of aging", "Only a brain MRI to rule out a structural lesion causing the falls"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **AGS/BGS Clinical Practice Guidelines for Fall Prevention** recommend a **multifactorial fall risk assessment** for all older adults who report **>= 2 falls in 12 months, a single fall with injury, or gait/balance difficulties**. The assessment should include: (1) **Gait, balance, and mobility** (Timed Up and Go [TUG], Berg Balance Scale), (2) **Orthostatic blood pressure** (supine to standing), (3) **Visual acuity** (cataracts, macular degeneration, glaucoma), (4) **Medication review** (especially **CNS-active drugs**: benzodiazepines, opioids, anticonvulsants, antidepressants; antihypertensives causing orthostasis), (5) **Lower extremity strength, proprioception, and sensation** (peripheral neuropathy), (6) **Footwear evaluation**, (7) **Cognitive screening** (dementia increases fall risk 2-3x), (8) **Home safety/hazard assessment** (loose rugs, poor lighting, absence of grab bars), (9) **Cardiovascular assessment** (arrhythmias, carotid sinus hypersensitivity). (Ref: AGS/BGS 2010 Clinical Practice Guideline; Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old woman who lives alone has had 5 falls in the past year, primarily occurring inside her home. An occupational therapist is consulted for a home safety evaluation. Which of the following home modification strategies has the strongest evidence for reducing fall risk in community-dwelling older adults?', '["Remove all furniture from the living room to create an open space for walking", "Install hospital-grade bed rails on all beds to prevent the patient from getting up at night", "Install wall-to-wall carpeting throughout the house to cushion any future falls", "Remove loose rugs and clutter from walkways, install grab bars in the bathroom and near the toilet, ensure adequate lighting (especially nightlights in hallways and bathrooms), install handrails on all staircases, use non-slip mats in the shower/tub, and ensure the bed height allows easy transfers", "No home modifications are effective; the patient should be moved to an assisted living facility immediately"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Home safety modification** is a key component of multifactorial fall prevention. A Cochrane review demonstrated that **home hazard assessment and modification by occupational therapists reduces fall rates by approximately 20-30%** in community-dwelling older adults. Evidence-based modifications include: (1) **Remove tripping hazards** (loose rugs, electrical cords, clutter), (2) **Install grab bars** in the bathroom (near toilet, in shower/tub), (3) **Adequate lighting** (nightlights in hallways, bathrooms, bedrooms), (4) **Handrails on all staircases** (bilateral if possible), (5) **Non-slip mats/strips** in bathtubs and showers, (6) **Raised toilet seats** and shower chairs for patients with mobility limitations, (7) **Secure or remove loose carpeting**, (8) **Appropriate bed height** for easy transfers. Bed rails are NOT recommended as they increase the risk of entrapment injury, particularly in cognitively impaired patients. (Ref: Cochrane Review, Clemson 2008; AGS/BGS Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
