    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old woman who lives alone has had 5 falls in the past year, primarily occurring inside her home. An occupational therapist is consulted for a home safety evaluation. Which of the following home modification strategies has the strongest evidence for reducing fall risk in community-dwelling older adults?', '["Install wall-to-wall carpeting throughout the house to cushion any future falls", "Remove all furniture from the living room to create an open space for walking", "No home modifications are effective; the patient should be moved to an assisted living facility immediately", "Remove loose rugs and clutter from walkways, install grab bars in the bathroom and near the toilet, ensure adequate lighting (especially nightlights in hallways and bathrooms), install handrails on all staircases, use non-slip mats in the shower/tub, and ensure the bed height allows easy transfers", "Install hospital-grade bed rails on all beds to prevent the patient from getting up at night"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Home safety modification** is a key component of multifactorial fall prevention. A Cochrane review demonstrated that **home hazard assessment and modification by occupational therapists reduces fall rates by approximately 20-30%** in community-dwelling older adults. Evidence-based modifications include: (1) **Remove tripping hazards** (loose rugs, electrical cords, clutter), (2) **Install grab bars** in the bathroom (near toilet, in shower/tub), (3) **Adequate lighting** (nightlights in hallways, bathrooms, bedrooms), (4) **Handrails on all staircases** (bilateral if possible), (5) **Non-slip mats/strips** in bathtubs and showers, (6) **Raised toilet seats** and shower chairs for patients with mobility limitations, (7) **Secure or remove loose carpeting**, (8) **Appropriate bed height** for easy transfers. Bed rails are NOT recommended as they increase the risk of entrapment injury, particularly in cognitively impaired patients. (Ref: Cochrane Review, Clemson 2008; AGS/BGS Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old female with hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and Parkinson disease reports recurrent falls, primarily occurring when she stands up from a seated or lying position. Blood pressure measured supine is 162/81 mmHg, and upon standing it drops to 134/62 mmHg with dizziness. Which of the following is the most accurate diagnosis and the most appropriate initial management?', '["Essential hypertension with white coat effect; increase the antihypertensive doses because the supine blood pressure is elevated", "Orthostatic hypotension (drop of >= 20 mmHg systolic or >= 10 mmHg diastolic within 3 minutes of standing); management includes reviewing and reducing antihypertensive doses, adequate hydration, rising slowly from supine/seated positions, compression stockings, and considering midodrine or fludrocortisone if non-pharmacologic measures fail", "Vasovagal syncope; prescribe a beta-blocker to prevent future episodes", "Carotid sinus hypersensitivity; immediately refer for pacemaker implantation", "Postprandial hypotension; restrict all meals to once daily to prevent blood pressure drops"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Orthostatic hypotension (OH)** is defined as a sustained drop of **>= 20 mmHg systolic or >= 10 mmHg diastolic** blood pressure within **3 minutes of standing** from a supine position. It is a major reversible cause of falls in older adults and is highly prevalent in patients with **autonomic neuropathy** (diabetes, Parkinson disease), **volume depletion**, and **medication-related causes** (antihypertensives, alpha-blockers, tricyclic antidepressants, diuretics). Management: (1) **Review and reduce/discontinue offending medications** (most important step), (2) **Adequate hydration** (salt and fluid intake), (3) **Postural precautions** (rise slowly, sit at bedside before standing, avoid prolonged standing), (4) **Compression stockings** (thigh-high or abdominal binders), (5) If refractory: **midodrine** (alpha-1 agonist) or **fludrocortisone** (mineralocorticoid). (Ref: AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Consensus Statement, JACC 2018)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old woman with hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and Parkinson disease reports recurrent falls, primarily occurring when she stands up from a seated or lying position. Blood pressure measured supine is 147/79 mmHg, and upon standing it drops to 112/65 mmHg with dizziness. Which of the following is the most accurate diagnosis and the most appropriate initial management?', '["Orthostatic hypotension (drop of >= 20 mmHg systolic or >= 10 mmHg diastolic within 3 minutes of standing); management includes reviewing and reducing antihypertensive doses, adequate hydration, rising slowly from supine/seated positions, compression stockings, and considering midodrine or fludrocortisone if non-pharmacologic measures fail", "Postprandial hypotension; restrict all meals to once daily to prevent blood pressure drops", "Essential hypertension with white coat effect; increase the antihypertensive doses because the supine blood pressure is elevated", "Vasovagal syncope; prescribe a beta-blocker to prevent future episodes", "Carotid sinus hypersensitivity; immediately refer for pacemaker implantation"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Orthostatic hypotension (OH)** is defined as a sustained drop of **>= 20 mmHg systolic or >= 10 mmHg diastolic** blood pressure within **3 minutes of standing** from a supine position. It is a major reversible cause of falls in older adults and is highly prevalent in patients with **autonomic neuropathy** (diabetes, Parkinson disease), **volume depletion**, and **medication-related causes** (antihypertensives, alpha-blockers, tricyclic antidepressants, diuretics). Management: (1) **Review and reduce/discontinue offending medications** (most important step), (2) **Adequate hydration** (salt and fluid intake), (3) **Postural precautions** (rise slowly, sit at bedside before standing, avoid prolonged standing), (4) **Compression stockings** (thigh-high or abdominal binders), (5) If refractory: **midodrine** (alpha-1 agonist) or **fludrocortisone** (mineralocorticoid). (Ref: AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Consensus Statement, JACC 2018)'),
