    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old male presents with a low-trauma vertebral compression fracture. He has a history of long-term androgen deprivation therapy for prostate cancer. A DEXA scan reveals a T-score of -2.8 at the lumbar spine. Which of the following statements is most accurate regarding osteoporosis in men?', '["Osteoporosis does not occur in men because testosterone completely protects bone density throughout life", "Testosterone replacement alone is sufficient treatment for all male osteoporosis regardless of the underlying cause", "Only women should be treated for osteoporosis; male bone loss is always benign and self-limited", "Osteoporosis in men is under-recognized; secondary causes account for up to 50-80% of male osteoporosis and must be systematically excluded (hypogonadism, glucocorticoid use, alcohol abuse, antiandrogen therapy); treatment includes bisphosphonates, denosumab, or teriparatide, with the same T-score diagnostic thresholds as for women aged >= 50", "DEXA T-score criteria cannot be applied to men; a bone biopsy is required for diagnosis in all males"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Male osteoporosis** is significantly **under-recognized and under-treated**. Key facts: (1) About **30% of all hip fractures** occur in men, and men have **higher post-hip fracture mortality** than women. (2) **Secondary causes** account for **50-80%** of male osteoporosis (vs. ~30% in women). Common secondary causes include: **hypogonadism** (most common), **glucocorticoid use**, **excessive alcohol use** (>3 drinks/day), **androgen deprivation therapy** for prostate cancer, **malabsorption syndromes**, **hyperparathyroidism**, and **hyperthyroidism**. (3) WHO T-score criteria apply to men **>= 50 years** (osteoporosis = T-score <= -2.5). For men < 50, the Z-score is preferred. (4) Treatment: bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, zoledronic acid), denosumab, and teriparatide are all FDA-approved for male osteoporosis. Testosterone replacement may be considered for hypogonadal men but is NOT sufficient as sole therapy if severe osteoporosis/fractures are present. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Endocrine Society Guidelines 2012; AACE/ACE 2020)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 61-year-old male presents with a low-trauma vertebral compression fracture. He has a history of chronic prednisone use (10 mg/day for 5 years) for COPD. A DEXA scan reveals a T-score of -2.8 at the lumbar spine. Which of the following statements is most accurate regarding osteoporosis in men?', '["Osteoporosis in men is under-recognized; secondary causes account for up to 50-80% of male osteoporosis and must be systematically excluded (hypogonadism, glucocorticoid use, alcohol abuse, antiandrogen therapy); treatment includes bisphosphonates, denosumab, or teriparatide, with the same T-score diagnostic thresholds as for women aged >= 50", "Osteoporosis does not occur in men because testosterone completely protects bone density throughout life", "Only women should be treated for osteoporosis; male bone loss is always benign and self-limited", "Testosterone replacement alone is sufficient treatment for all male osteoporosis regardless of the underlying cause", "DEXA T-score criteria cannot be applied to men; a bone biopsy is required for diagnosis in all males"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Male osteoporosis** is significantly **under-recognized and under-treated**. Key facts: (1) About **30% of all hip fractures** occur in men, and men have **higher post-hip fracture mortality** than women. (2) **Secondary causes** account for **50-80%** of male osteoporosis (vs. ~30% in women). Common secondary causes include: **hypogonadism** (most common), **glucocorticoid use**, **excessive alcohol use** (>3 drinks/day), **androgen deprivation therapy** for prostate cancer, **malabsorption syndromes**, **hyperparathyroidism**, and **hyperthyroidism**. (3) WHO T-score criteria apply to men **>= 50 years** (osteoporosis = T-score <= -2.5). For men < 50, the Z-score is preferred. (4) Treatment: bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, zoledronic acid), denosumab, and teriparatide are all FDA-approved for male osteoporosis. Testosterone replacement may be considered for hypogonadal men but is NOT sufficient as sole therapy if severe osteoporosis/fractures are present. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Endocrine Society Guidelines 2012; AACE/ACE 2020)'),
