    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old male presents with a low-trauma vertebral compression fracture. He has a history of long-term androgen deprivation therapy for prostate cancer. A DEXA scan reveals a T-score of -2.8 at the lumbar spine. Which of the following statements is most accurate regarding osteoporosis in men?', '["Osteoporosis in men is under-recognized; secondary causes account for up to 50-80% of male osteoporosis and must be systematically excluded (hypogonadism, glucocorticoid use, alcohol abuse, antiandrogen therapy); treatment includes bisphosphonates, denosumab, or teriparatide, with the same T-score diagnostic thresholds as for women aged >= 50", "Osteoporosis does not occur in men because testosterone completely protects bone density throughout life", "Testosterone replacement alone is sufficient treatment for all male osteoporosis regardless of the underlying cause", "Only women should be treated for osteoporosis; male bone loss is always benign and self-limited", "DEXA T-score criteria cannot be applied to men; a bone biopsy is required for diagnosis in all males"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Male osteoporosis** is significantly **under-recognized and under-treated**. Key facts: (1) About **30% of all hip fractures** occur in men, and men have **higher post-hip fracture mortality** than women. (2) **Secondary causes** account for **50-80%** of male osteoporosis (vs. ~30% in women). Common secondary causes include: **hypogonadism** (most common), **glucocorticoid use**, **excessive alcohol use** (>3 drinks/day), **androgen deprivation therapy** for prostate cancer, **malabsorption syndromes**, **hyperparathyroidism**, and **hyperthyroidism**. (3) WHO T-score criteria apply to men **>= 50 years** (osteoporosis = T-score <= -2.5). For men < 50, the Z-score is preferred. (4) Treatment: bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, zoledronic acid), denosumab, and teriparatide are all FDA-approved for male osteoporosis. Testosterone replacement may be considered for hypogonadal men but is NOT sufficient as sole therapy if severe osteoporosis/fractures are present. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Endocrine Society Guidelines 2012; AACE/ACE 2020)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old postmenopausal female is diagnosed with osteoporosis (T-score -2.9 at the femoral neck). She has no contraindications to any osteoporosis medication. She asks about the overall treatment approach and the sequence of available therapies. Which of the following most accurately describes the current evidence-based treatment algorithm for postmenopausal osteoporosis?', '["Denosumab is always first-line because it has the fewest side effects of any osteoporosis medication", "Raloxifene is the preferred first-line agent for all postmenopausal women with osteoporosis because it reduces both vertebral and hip fracture risk", "First-line: oral bisphosphonate (alendronate or risedronate) plus calcium and vitamin D for all patients; IV zoledronic acid if oral bisphosphonates are contraindicated or not tolerated; denosumab as an alternative anti-resorptive; teriparatide or romosozumab reserved for very high-risk patients (very low T-score, multiple fractures, treatment failure); raloxifene considered if concurrent breast cancer risk reduction is desired", "Hormone replacement therapy (HRT) with estrogen plus progesterone is the recommended first-line treatment for all postmenopausal osteoporosis", "Teriparatide is always first-line for all patients with osteoporosis regardless of severity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The evidence-based **treatment algorithm for postmenopausal osteoporosis**: **First-line**: Oral bisphosphonates (**alendronate** or **risedronate**) are first-line for most patients due to strong fracture reduction evidence (vertebral, non-vertebral, and hip), long track record, generic availability, and cost-effectiveness. **Calcium (1200 mg/day) and vitamin D (800-1000 IU/day)** are adjunctive for all patients. **Second-line/alternatives**: (1) **IV zoledronic acid** - for patients who cannot tolerate or are contraindicated for oral bisphosphonates. (2) **Denosumab** - for patients with renal insufficiency (eGFR < 35 mL/min, where bisphosphonates are relatively contraindicated), intolerance to bisphosphonates, or preference for SC injection. (3) **Raloxifene** - for patients with concurrent high breast cancer risk (reduces only vertebral fractures). **Reserved for very high risk**: (4) **Teriparatide** or **abaloparatide** (anabolic agents) - for very low T-scores, multiple fractures, or treatment failure. (5) **Romosozumab** - for very high fracture risk without CV contraindications. **HRT** is no longer first-line due to cardiovascular and cancer risks (WHI Trial). (Ref: NOF Clinician''s Guide; AACE/ACE 2020; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Endocrine Society)'),
